<nav aria-label="Main navigation"><a href="../getting-started.html">Start learning</a><a href="index.html">Wiki</a><a href="../source-map.html">Source map</a><a href="../products.html">Applications</a><a href="../downloads.html">Downloads</a><a href="https://github.com/umicom-foundation">GitHub</a></nav></header>
<main id="content"><header class="hero"><p class="eyebrow">Umicom Framework</p><h1>Compare two texts without changing either one</h1><p class="lead">Use immutable snapshots, the shared line-diff engine and a reusable read-only component to review a saved document.</p></header>
<p>A comparison answers a question: what differs between these two versions? It should not decide which version to keep. Framework separates the comparison model from document reload policy so the same component can be used in Notes, a source editor or a configuration viewer.</p>
<p>For the Studio workflow, a reload plan captures the current editor draft and the incoming saved text. The comparison reads those exact snapshots. Only a separate, approved reload can replace the draft. See <a href="reloading-files.html">Reloading documents</a> for the state and revision checks, and the <a href="../studio/COMPARING_SAVED_FILES.html">Studio lesson</a> for the user workflow.</p>
<h2>Find the code and its owning targets</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Responsibility</th><th>Location</th></tr>
<tr><td>Immutable comparison model</td><td><code>include/umicom/developer_productivity/text_comparison.h</code><br><code>src/developer_productivity/text_comparison.c</code><br>Target: <code>Umicom::developer</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>Existing line alignment and navigation</td><td><code>src/developer_productivity/diff.c</code>, <code>diff_navigation.c</code>, <code>diff_hunk.c</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>Captured reload-plan text</td><td><code>include/umicom/document/coordinator.h</code><br><code>src/document/coordinator.c</code><br>Target: <code>Umicom::document</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>Read-only GTK component</td><td><code>include/umicom/ui/gtk4/text_comparison.h</code><br><code>adapters/gtk4/text_comparison_gtk4.c</code><br>Target: <code>Umicom::ui_gtk4</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>Studio presentation</td><td><code>applications/studio/src/gui/workbench/runtime/interaction/editor_controls.inc</code><br><code>confirm_dialog.inc</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>Examples and tests</td><td><code>examples/editor_workflow/review.c</code><br><code>tests/developer_productivity/test_text_comparison.c</code><br><code>tests/developer_productivity/test_text_comparison_gtk4.c</code><br><code>tests/document/test_reload.c</code></td></tr></table></div>
<p>The headless model reuses the existing diff engine; it does not parse C source, perform semantic renaming or create a second document store. The GTK adapter knows nothing about provider access or permission to reload. Studio supplies the specific document and the confirmation.</p>
<h2>Read the contracts before creating the view</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Meaning and ownership</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>UmiDocumentReloadPlanTexts</code></td><td>Borrows both complete texts from an unconsumed reload plan. All four outputs are required. They are cleared on an error. Do not modify or free the returned pointers; successful Apply or plan destruction ends their lifetime.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>UmiTextComparisonCreate</code></td><td>Copies two NUL-free, terminated text spans. Explicit lengths exclude the final zero byte. On success the caller owns one model; on failure the output remains NULL.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>UmiTextComparisonGetSummary</code></td><td>Copies byte counts, physical line counts, exact byte identity, final-newline flags, aligned row count, change counts and alignment status.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>UmiTextComparisonText</code></td><td>Borrows the model's full left (0) or right (1) text until model destruction. Both output pointers are required and are cleared on an error.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>UmiTextComparisonRow</code></td><td>Copies one aligned row. Its source line numbers start at 1; zero means that side has no source line. Row text excludes line endings.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>UmiTextComparisonNavigate</code></td><td>Finds a changed row with direction +1 or -1. Start with <code>SIZE_MAX</code> to search forward from the beginning or backwards from the end. Navigation does not wrap.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>UmiTextComparisonDestroy</code></td><td>Frees the model, owned text copies and optional alignment. Passing NULL is permitted.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>UmiGtk4TextComparisonCreate</code></td><td>Requires valid UTF-8 and creates a floating GTK widget. Add it to a container, or sink and release its reference explicitly. Use only on the GTK owner thread.</td></tr></table></div>
<p>An unconsumed plan owns its texts independently of the coordinator, but it must never be applied to a destroyed or replacement coordinator. A comparison model makes independent copies, so it may outlive the plan. Keep that distinction explicit in cleanup code.</p>
<h2>Alignment is not the same as byte equality</h2>
<p>The diff engine aligns matching lines using a longest common subsequence. For example, the versions <code>Account / Balance</code> and <code>Balance / Account</code> still share an unchanged line, even though it appears at another position. An alignment should retain that equality rather than claim that both lines were rewritten.</p>
<p>Within each changed block, removed and added lines are paired as replacements where possible. Equal-score choices are resolved consistently; equivalent optimal alignments may exist. Changed rows are a presentation of a line comparison, not an executable patch or a measurement of code semantics.</p>
<p>Line comparison normalises line terminators. The model therefore also reports exact byte identity and whether each text ends in a newline. Zero changed rows alone cannot prove that two inputs are byte-for-byte identical. A reload plan has already decoded its saved input into the editor's UTF-8/LF representation, so it is not a raw encoding or BOM comparison.</p>
<h2>Limits and failure behaviour</h2>
<p>Each complete text may contain up to 8 MiB. The existing alignment engine accepts at most 512 lines per side, with at most 1,023 bytes in a line. Exceeding the alignment bound leaves a valid full-text model with <code>alignmentStatus = UMI_STATUS_CAPACITY_EXCEEDED</code>. The caller can still read both complete texts. Row access and change navigation return that alignment status rather than pretending there are no differences.</p>
<p>Allocation failures and other alignment errors fail model creation and do not publish a partial object. NULL inputs, a mismatched terminator or an embedded NUL are invalid. The byte model does not validate UTF-8; the GTK adapter does, before displaying it. This is a text viewer, not a binary-file comparison tool.</p>
<p>The model holds two full text copies plus the bounded alignment data. A prepared reload plan and GTK buffers have their own storage, so the per-text bound is not a total application-memory ceiling. Construction is synchronous. Reading a slow provider or aligning a document can delay the owner thread; there is no streaming or background comparison job in this interface.</p>
<h2>Build and run a complete Notes review</h2>
<p>The example below owns a new temporary Notes file. It changes the draft and the saved version independently, prints the changed row and confirms that review did not alter the draft. It then checks refusal without approval, accepts its own temporary reload, and restores the earlier draft with Undo. A real application must obtain a user's approval rather than copying the exercise's deliberate acceptance.</p>
<pre><code class="language-c">@UMICOM_SITE_REVIEW_EXAMPLE@</code></pre>
<p>Install a Framework SDK first. From the Applications root, build the examples against that installation; replace the prefix only when your SDK is installed elsewhere.</p>
<h3>Windows PowerShell</h3>
<pre><code>Set-Location "C:\umicom\Umicom-Applications"
$env:Path = "C:\msys64\ucrt64\bin;$env:Path"
cmake -S framework/examples/editor_workflow -B build/editor-review-example -G Ninja -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH="C:/umicom/installed/development"
cmake --build build/editor-review-example --parallel 2
ctest --test-dir build/editor-review-example --no-tests=error --output-on-failure
&amp; ".\build\editor-review-example\umicom-notes-review.exe"</code></pre>
<h3>Linux</h3>
<pre><code>cd "$HOME/umicom/Umicom-Applications"
cmake -S framework/examples/editor_workflow -B build/editor-review-example -G Ninja -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH="$HOME/umicom/installed/development"
cmake --build build/editor-review-example --parallel 2
ctest --test-dir build/editor-review-example --no-tests=error --output-on-failure
./build/editor-review-example/umicom-notes-review</code></pre>
<p>Both commands build the actual example source against installed targets. A failure to find <code>UmicomFrameworkConfig.cmake</code> means the SDK prefix needs checking; adding private source include paths is not the fix.</p>
<h2>Connect the reusable GTK component</h2>
<pre><code class="language-c">GtkWidget *review = NULL;
UmiStatus status = UmiGtk4TextComparisonCreate(
    draft, draftLength, saved, savedLength,
    "Current editor draft", "Saved version", &amp;review);
if (status == UMI_STATUS_OK) {
    gtk_box_append(GTK_BOX(parentBox), review);
}</code></pre>
<p>This fragment assumes initialised GTK, a valid parent box and two readable, terminated UTF-8 spans. It displays the texts but does not authorise or apply a reload. Keep the prepared plan in the dialog's owned context, use Cancel as the default, and call the existing revision-checked apply operation only after the intended approval.</p>
<p>Difference buttons select the relevant source lines in each pane. The panes retain independent scrolling and source order; there is no automatic hunk merge or permanent row-padding grid. Child signals are tied to the component root so a retained button does not use the model after the root is destroyed.</p>
<h2>Extend the exercise</h2>
<p>Change only the final newline, then inspect byte identity and changed-row count. Next, add 600 short lines and verify that complete text remains readable while alignment reports its limit. Finally, prepare a plan, change the saved resource and attempt to apply the old plan: the read-only model is still a useful historical view, but that old approval must not replace newer state.</p>
